## an3.120
an3.120:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three kinds of purity. | T: Monks, there are these three purities.
an3.120:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.120:1.3 | S: Purity of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily purity, verbal purity, & mental purity.
an3.120:1.4 | S: And what is purity of body? | T: “And what is bodily purity?
an3.120:1.5 | S: It’s when someone doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, or commit sexual misconduct. | T: There is the case where a certain person abstains from taking life, abstains from theft, abstains from sexual misconduct.
an3.120:1.6 | S: This is called ‘purity of body’. | T: This is called bodily purity.
an3.120:2.1 | S: And what is purity of speech? | T: “And what is verbal purity?
an3.120:2.2 | S: It’s when someone doesn’t use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical. | T: There is the case where a certain person abstains from telling lies, abstains from divisive speech, abstains from harsh speech, abstains from idle chatter.
an3.120:2.3 | S: This is called ‘purity of speech’. | T: This is called verbal purity.
an3.120:3.1 | S: And what is purity of mind? | T: “And what is mental purity?
an3.120:3.2 | S: It’s when someone is content, kind-hearted, and has right view. | T: There is the case where a certain person is one who is not covetous, is one whose mind is devoid of ill will, and is one with right view.
an3.120:3.3 | S: This is called ‘purity of mind’. | T: This is called mental purity.
an3.120:3.4 | S: These are the three kinds of purity.” | T: “These, monks, are the three purities.”
an3.120:3.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.121
an3.121:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three kinds of purity. | T: Monks, there are these three purities.
an3.121:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.121:1.3 | S: Purity of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily purity, verbal purity, & mental purity.
an3.121:1.4 | S: And what is purity of body? | T: “And what is bodily purity?
an3.121:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, or have sex. | T: There is the case where a monk abstains from taking life, abstains from theft, abstains from uncelibacy.
an3.121:1.6 | S: This is called ‘purity of body’. | T: This is called bodily purity.
an3.121:2.1 | S: And what is purity of speech? | T: “And what is verbal purity?
an3.121:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical. | T: There is the case where a monk abstains from telling lies, abstains from divisive speech, abstains from harsh speech, abstains from idle chatter.
an3.121:2.3 | S: This is called ‘purity of speech’. | T: This is called verbal purity.
an3.121:3.1 | S: And what is purity of mind? | T: “And what is mental purity?
an3.121:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant who has sensual desire in them understands ‘I have sensual desire in me.’ When they don’t have sensual desire in them, they understand ‘I don’t have sensual desire in me.’ They understand how sensual desire arises; how, when it’s already arisen, it’s given up; and how, once it’s given up, it doesn’t arise again in the future. | T: There is the case where, there being sensual desire present within, a monk discerns, ‘There is sensual desire present within me.’ Or, there being no sensual desire present within, he discerns, ‘There is no sensual desire present within me.’ He discerns how there is the arising of unarisen sensual desire. And he discerns how there is the abandoning of sensual desire once it has arisen. And he discerns how there is no further appearance in the future of sensual desire that has been abandoned.
an3.121:3.3 | S: When they have ill will in them they understand ‘I have ill will in me’; and when they don’t have ill will in them they understand ‘I don’t have ill will in me’. They understand how ill will arises; how, when it’s already arisen, it’s given up; and how, once it’s given up, it doesn’t arise again in the future. | T: “There being ill will present within…
an3.121:3.4 | S: When they have dullness and drowsiness in them they understand ‘I have dullness and drowsiness in me’; and when they don’t have dullness and drowsiness in them they understand ‘I don’t have dullness and drowsiness in me’. They understand how dullness and drowsiness arise; how, when they’ve already arisen, they’re given up; and how, once they’re given up, they don’t arise again in the future. | T: “There being sloth & drowsiness present within…
an3.121:3.5 | S: When they have restlessness and remorse in them they understand ‘I have restlessness and remorse in me’; and when they don’t have restlessness and remorse in them they understand ‘I don’t have restlessness and remorse in me’. They understand how restlessness and remorse arise; how, when they’ve already arisen, they’re given up; and how, once they’re given up, they don’t arise again in the future. | T: “There being restlessness & anxiety present within…
an3.121:3.6 | S: When they have doubt in them they understand ‘I have doubt in me’; and when they don’t have doubt in them they understand ‘I don’t have doubt in me’. They understand how doubt arises; how, when it’s already arisen, it’s given up; and how, once it’s given up, it doesn’t arise again in the future. | T: “There being uncertainty present within, a monk discerns, ‘There is uncertainty present within me.’ Or, there being no uncertainty present within, he discerns, ‘There is no uncertainty present within me.’ He discerns how there is the arising of unarisen uncertainty. And he discerns how there is the abandoning of uncertainty once it has arisen. And he discerns how there is no further appearance in the future of uncertainty that has been abandoned.
an3.121:3.7 | S: This is called ‘purity of mind’. | T: This is called mental purity.”
an3.121:3.8 | S: These are the three kinds of purity. | T: “These, monks, are the three purities.”
an3.121:4.1 | S: Purity of body, purity of speech, | T: Bodily pure, verbally pure,
an3.121:4.2 | S: and undefiled purity of heart. | T: mentally pure, without effluent:
an3.121:4.3 | S: A pure one, blessed with purity, | T: A pure one, consummate in purity,
an3.121:4.4 | S: has washed off all bad things, they say.” | T: is said to be cleansed of evil.
an3.121:4.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.122
an3.122:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three kinds of sagacity. | T: “Monks, there are these three forms of sagacity.
an3.122:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.122:1.3 | S: Sagacity of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily sagacity, verbal sagacity, & mental sagacity.
an3.122:1.4 | S: And what is sagacity of body? | T: “And what is bodily sagacity?
an3.122:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, or have sex. | T: There is the case where a monk abstains from taking life, abstains from theft, abstains from uncelibacy.
an3.122:1.6 | S: This is called ‘sagacity of body’. | T: This is called bodily sagacity.
an3.122:2.1 | S: And what is sagacity of speech? | T: “And what is verbal sagacity?
an3.122:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical. | T: There is the case where a monk abstains from telling lies, abstains from divisive speech, abstains from harsh speech, abstains from idle chatter.
an3.122:2.3 | S: This is called ‘sagacity of speech’. | T: This is called verbal sagacity.
an3.122:3.1 | S: And what is sagacity of mind? | T: “And what is mental sagacity?
an3.122:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: There is the case where a monk who—with the ending of effluents—enters & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized it for himself right in the here & now.
an3.122:3.3 | S: This is called ‘sagacity of mind’. | T: This is called mental sagacity.
an3.122:3.4 | S: These are the three kinds of sagacity. | T: “These, monks, are the three forms of sagacity.”
an3.122:4.1 | S: A sage in body, a sage in speech, | T: A sage in body, a sage in speech,
an3.122:4.2 | S: a sage undefiled in heart; | T: a sage in mind, without effluent,
an3.122:4.3 | S: a sage, blessed with sagacity, | T: a sage consummate in sagacity
an3.122:4.4 | S: has given up everything, they say.” | T: is said to have abandoned everything —the All.
an3.122:4.5 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.122:4.6 | P: Āpāyikavaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
an3.122:5.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
