## an3.123
an3.123:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Kusinārā in the Forest of Offerings. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Kusināra in the Baliharaṇa [Oblation-taking] forest grove.
an3.123:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
an3.123:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
an3.123:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an3.123:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an3.123:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, take the case of a mendicant living supported by a town or village. | T: “There is the case where a monk lives in dependence on a certain town or village.
an3.123:2.2 | S: A householder or their child approaches and invites them for the next day’s meal. | T: A householder or householder’s son, approaching him, invites him for the next day’s meal.
an3.123:2.3 | S: The mendicant accepts if they want. | T: If he wants to, he acquiesces.
an3.123:2.4 | S: When the night has passed, they robe up in the morning, take their bowl and robe, and approach that householder’s home, where they sit on the seat spread out. | T: When the night has passed, early in the morning, he adjusts his under robe and—carrying his bowl & robes—goes to the residence of that householder or householder’s son. On arrival, he sits down on a seat laid out.
an3.123:2.5 | S: The householder or their child serves and satisfies them with their own hands with delicious fresh and cooked foods. | T: The householder or householder’s son, with his own hand, serves & satisfies the monk with exquisite staple & non-staple food.
an3.123:3.1 | S: The mendicant thinks: | T: The thought occurs to the monk,
an3.123:3.2 | S: ‘It’s so good that this householder serves me with delicious fresh and cooked foods.’ | T: ‘How good it is that this householder or householder’s son serves & satisfies me with exquisite staple & non-staple food!’
an3.123:3.3 | S: Then they think: | T: And the thought also occurs to him,
an3.123:3.4 | S: ‘I really hope this householder serves me with delicious fresh and cooked foods in the future, too.’ | T: ‘O, may this householder or householder’s son in the future serve & satisfy me with this sort of exquisite staple & non-staple food!’
an3.123:3.5 | S: They eat that food tied, infatuated, attached, blind to the drawbacks, and not understanding the escape. | T: He consumes that almsfood attached to it, infatuated, guilty, not seeing the drawbacks (of attachment to it), and not discerning the escape from them.
an3.123:3.6 | S: They think about it with sensual, malicious, or cruel thoughts. | T: With regard to it, he thinks thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, thoughts of harmfulness.
an3.123:3.7 | S: A gift to such a mendicant is not very fruitful, I say. | T: What is given to a monk of this sort, I tell you, is not of great fruit.
an3.123:3.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an3.123:3.9 | S: Because that mendicant is negligent. | T: Because the monk remains heedless.
an3.123:4.1 | S: Take another case of a mendicant living supported by a town or village. | T: “But there is the case where a monk lives in dependence on a certain town or village.
an3.123:4.2 | S: A householder or their child approaches and invites them for the next day’s meal. | T: A householder or householder’s son, approaching him, invites him for the next day’s meal.
an3.123:4.3 | S: The mendicant accepts if they want. | T: If he wants to, he acquiesces.
an3.123:4.4 | S: When the night has passed, they robe up in the morning, take their bowl and robe, and approach that householder’s home, where they sit on the seat spread out. | T: When the night has passed, early in the morning, he adjusts his under robe and—carrying his bowl & robes—goes to the residence of that householder or householder’s son. On arrival, he sits down on a seat laid out.
an3.123:4.5 | S: The householder or their child serves and satisfies them with their own hands with delicious fresh and cooked foods. | T: The householder or householder’s son, with his own hand, serves & satisfies the monk with exquisite staple & non-staple food.
an3.123:5.1 | S: It never occurs to them: | T: The thought doesn’t occur to the monk,
an3.123:5.2 | S: ‘It’s so good that the householder or their child serves and satisfies me with their own hands with delicious fresh and cooked foods.’ | T: ‘How good it is that this householder or householder’s son serves & satisfies me with exquisite staple & non-staple food!’
an3.123:5.3 | S: They don’t think: | T: And the thought also doesn’t occur to him,
an3.123:5.4 | S: ‘I really hope this householder serves me with delicious fresh and cooked foods in the future, too.’ | T: ‘O, may this householder or householder’s son in the future serve & satisfy me with this sort of exquisite staple & non-staple food!’
an3.123:5.5 | S: They eat that almsfood untied, uninfatuated, unattached, seeing the drawback, and understanding the escape. | T: He consumes that almsfood unattached to it, uninfatuated, guiltless, seeing the drawbacks (of attachment to it), and discerning the escape from them.
an3.123:5.6 | S: They think about it with thoughts of renunciation, good will, or harmlessness. | T: With regard to it, he thinks thoughts of renunciation, thoughts of non-ill will, thoughts of harmlessness.
an3.123:5.7 | S: A gift to such a mendicant is very fruitful, I say. | T: What is given to a monk of this sort, I tell you, is of great fruit.
an3.123:5.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an3.123:5.9 | S: Because that mendicant is diligent.” | T: Because the monk remains heedful.”
an3.123:5.10 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.125
an3.125:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Gotamaka Shrine. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesāli at Gotamaka Shrine.
an3.125:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
an3.125:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
an3.125:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an3.125:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an3.125:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, I teach based on direct knowledge, not without direct knowledge. | T: “Monks, it’s through direct knowledge that I teach the Dhamma, not without direct knowledge.
an3.125:2.2 | S: I teach with reasons, not without them. | T: It’s with a cause that I teach the Dhamma, not without a cause.
an3.125:2.3 | S: I teach with a demonstrable basis, not without it. | T: It’s with marvels that I teach the Dhamma, not without marvels.
an3.125:2.4 | S: Since this is so, you should follow my advice and instruction. | T: Because I teach the Dhamma through direct knowledge and not without direct knowledge, because I teach the Dhamma with a cause and not without a cause, because I teach the Dhamma with marvels and not without marvels, there is good reason for my instruction, good reason for my admonition.
an3.125:2.5 | S: This is enough for you to feel joyful, delighted, and happy: | T: And it is enough for you to be content, enough for you to be gratified, enough for you to take joy,
an3.125:2.6 | S: ‘The Blessed One is a fully awakened Buddha! The teaching is well explained! The Saṅgha is practicing well!’” | T: that the Blessed One is rightly self-awakened, the Dhamma is well-taught by the Blessed One, and the Saṅgha has practiced rightly.”
an3.125:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
an3.125:3.2 | S: Satisfied, the mendicants approved what the Buddha said. | T: Gratified, the monks delighted in the Blessed One’s words.
an3.125:3.3 | S: And while this discourse was being spoken, the thousandfold galaxy shook. | T: And while this explanation was being given, the ten-thousand-fold cosmos quaked.
an3.125:3.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.12
an3.12:1.1 | S: “An anointed aristocratic king should commemorate three places as long as he lives. | T: “Monks, these three things are to be remembered by a head-anointed noble-warrior king throughout life.
an3.12:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.12:1.3 | S: The place he was born. | T: “The place where he was born:
an3.12:1.4 | S: This is the first place. | T: This is the first thing that is to be remembered by a head-anointed noble-warrior king throughout life.
an3.12:2.1 | S: The place he was anointed as king. | T: “Then again, the place where he was head-anointed as a noble-warrior king:
an3.12:2.2 | S: This is the second place. | T: This is the second thing that is to be remembered by a head-anointed noble-warrior king throughout life.
an3.12:3.1 | S: The place where he won victory in battle, establishing himself as foremost in battle. | T: “Then again, the place where, having won the battle, he then emerged victorious and ruled at the head of the battle:
an3.12:3.2 | S: This is the third place. | T: This is the third thing that is to be remembered by a head-anointed noble-warrior king throughout life.
an3.12:3.3 | S: These are the three places an anointed king should commemorate as long as he lives. | T: “These, monks, are the three things to be remembered by a head-anointed noble-warrior king throughout life.
an3.12:4.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant should commemorate three places as long as they live. | T: “In the same way, these three things are to be remembered by a monk throughout life.
an3.12:4.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.12:4.3 | S: The place where the mendicant shaved off their hair and beard, dressed in ocher robes, and went forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: “The place where, shaving off his hair & beard, clothing himself in the ochre robe, he goes forth from the home life into homelessness:
an3.12:4.4 | S: This is the first place. | T: This is the first thing that is to be remembered by a monk throughout life.
an3.12:5.1 | S: The place where the mendicant truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: “Then again, the place where he discerns, as it has come to be, ‘This is stress’ … ‘This is the origination of stress’ … ‘This is the cessation of stress’ … ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress’:
an3.12:5.2 | S: This is the second place. | T: This is the second thing that is to be remembered by a monk throughout life.
an3.12:6.1 | S: The place where the mendicant realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: “Then again, the place where, through the ending of effluents, he enters & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, directly knowing & realizing it for himself right in the here-&-now:
an3.12:6.2 | S: This is the third place. | T: This is the third thing that is to be remembered by a monk throughout life.
an3.12:6.3 | S: These are the three places a mendicant should commemorate as long as they live.” | T: “These, monks, are the three things to be remembered by a monk throughout life.”
an3.12:6.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.130
an3.130:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Anuruddha went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Anuruddha went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an3.130:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to him: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an3.130:1.3 | S: “Here’s the thing, Reverend Sāriputta. With clairvoyance that is purified and surpasses the human, I survey the thousandfold galaxy. | T: “Here, by means of the divine eye, purified & surpassing the human, I see the thousand-fold cosmos.
an3.130:1.4 | S: My energy is roused up and unflagging, my mindfulness is established and lucid, my body is tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: And my persistence is aroused & unsluggish. My mindfulness is established & unmuddled. My body is calm & unaroused. My mind is concentrated & gathered into singleness.
an3.130:1.5 | S: But my mind is not freed from the defilements by not grasping.” | T: And yet my mind is not released from the effluents through lack of clinging/sustenance.”
an3.130:2.1 | S: “Well, Reverend Anuruddha, when you say: | T: Ven. Sāriputta: “My friend, when the thought occurs to you,
an3.130:2.2 | S: ‘With clairvoyance that is purified and surpasses the human, I survey the thousandfold galaxy,’ that’s your conceit. | T: ‘By means of the divine eye, purified & surpassing the human, I see the thousand-fold cosmos,’ that is related to your conceit.
an3.130:2.3 | S: And when you say: | T: When the thought occurs to you,
an3.130:2.4 | S: ‘My energy is roused up and unflagging, my mindfulness is established and lucid, my body is tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind is immersed in samādhi,’ that’s your restlessness. | T: ‘My persistence is aroused & unsluggish. My mindfulness is established & unmuddled. My body is calm & unaroused. My mind is concentrated & gathered into singleness,’ that is related to your restlessness.
an3.130:2.5 | S: And when you say: | T: When the thought occurs to you,
an3.130:2.6 | S: ‘But my mind is not freed from the defilements by not grasping,’ that’s your remorse. | T: ‘And yet my mind is not released from the effluents through lack of clinging/sustenance,’ that is related to your anxiety.
an3.130:2.7 | S: It would be good to give up these three things. Ignore them and apply your mind to freedom from death.” | T: It would be well if—abandoning these three qualities, not attending to these three qualities—you directed your mind to the deathless property.”
an3.130:3.1 | S: After some time Anuruddha gave up these three things. Ignoring them, he applied his mind to freedom from death. | T: So after that, Ven. Anuruddha—abandoning those three qualities, not attending to those three qualities—directed his mind to the deathless property.
an3.130:3.2 | S: Then Anuruddha, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute, he in no long time entered & remained in the unexcelled goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
an3.130:3.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew, “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
an3.130:3.4 | S: And Venerable Anuruddha became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Ven. Anuruddha became another one of the arahants.
an3.130:3.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

