## an3.136
an3.136:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, whether Realized Ones arise or not, this law of nature persists, this regularity of natural principles, this surety of natural principles: | T: I have heard that at one time the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. There he addressed the monks, saying, “Monks.” “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him. The Blessed One said, “Whether or not there is the arising of Tathāgatas, this property stands—this steadfastness of the Dhamma, this orderliness of the Dhamma:
an3.136:1.2 | S: all conditions are impermanent. | T: ‘All fabrications are inconstant.’
an3.136:1.3 | S: A Realized One awakens to this and comprehends it, | T: “The Tathāgata directly awakens to that, breaks through to that.
an3.136:1.4 | S: then he explains, teaches, asserts, establishes, clarifies, analyzes, and discloses it: | T: Directly awakening & breaking through to that, he declares it, teaches it, describes it, sets it forth. He reveals it, explains it, makes it plain:
an3.136:1.5 | S: ‘All conditions are impermanent.’ | T: ‘All fabrications are inconstant.’
an3.136:1.6 | S: Whether Realized Ones arise or not, this law of nature persists, this regularity of natural principles, this surety of natural principles: | T: “Whether or not there is the arising of Tathāgatas, this property stands—this steadfastness of the Dhamma, this orderliness of the Dhamma:
an3.136:1.7 | S: all conditions are suffering. | T: ‘All fabrications are stressful.’
an3.136:1.8 | S: A Realized One awakens to this and comprehends it, | T: “The Tathāgata directly awakens to that, breaks through to that.
an3.136:1.9 | S: then he explains, teaches, asserts, establishes, clarifies, analyzes, and discloses it: | T: Directly awakening & breaking through to that, he declares it, teaches it, describes it, sets it forth. He reveals it, explains it, makes it plain:
an3.136:1.10 | S: ‘All conditions are suffering.’ | T: ‘All fabrications are stressful.’
an3.136:1.11 | S: Whether Realized Ones arise or not, this law of nature persists, this regularity of natural principles, this surety of natural principles: | T: “Whether or not there is the arising of Tathāgatas, this property stands—this steadfastness of the Dhamma, this orderliness of the Dhamma:
an3.136:1.12 | S: all things are not-self. | T: ‘All phenomena are not-self.’
an3.136:1.13 | S: A Realized One awakens to this and comprehends it, | T: “The Tathāgata directly awakens to that, breaks through to that.
an3.136:1.14 | S: then he explains, teaches, asserts, establishes, clarifies, analyzes, and discloses it: | T: Directly awakening & breaking through to that, he declares it, teaches it, describes it, sets it forth. He reveals it, explains it, makes it plain:
an3.136:1.15 | S: ‘All things are not-self.’” | T: ‘All phenomena are not-self.’” That is what the Blessed One said. Gratified, the monks delighted at his words.
an3.136:1.16 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.1
an3.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an3.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta's Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an3.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks:
an3.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
an3.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an3.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an3.1:2.1 | S: “Whatever dangers there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: “Monks, whatever dangers arise all arise from a fool, and not from a wise person.
an3.1:2.2 | S: Whatever perils there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever disasters arise all arise from a fool, and not from a wise person.
an3.1:2.3 | S: Whatever hazards there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever troubles arise all arise from a fool, and not from a wise person.
an3.1:2.4 | S: It’s like a fire that spreads from a hut made of reeds or grass, and burns down even a bungalow, plastered inside and out, draft-free, with doors fastened and windows shuttered. | T: “Just as a fire, released from a house of reeds or a house of grass, burns even gabled houses, plastered inside & out, draft-free, with close-fitting doors & windows shut against the wind;
an3.1:2.5 | S: In the same way, whatever dangers there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: in the same way, whatever dangers arise all arise from a fool, and not from a wise person.
an3.1:2.6 | S: Whatever perils there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever disasters arise all arise from a fool, and not from a wise person.
an3.1:2.7 | S: Whatever hazards there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever troubles arise all arise from a fool and not from a wise person.
an3.1:3.1 | S: So, the fool is dangerous, but the astute person is safe. | T: “Thus, monks, a fool brings danger; a wise person brings no danger.
an3.1:3.2 | S: The fool is perilous, but the astute person is not. | T: A fool brings disasters; a wise person brings no disasters.
an3.1:3.3 | S: The fool is hazardous, but the astute person is not. | T: A fool brings trouble; a wise person brings no trouble.
an3.1:3.4 | S: There’s no danger, peril, or hazard that comes from the astute. | T: “There is no danger from a wise person. There are no disasters from a wise person. There is no trouble from a wise person.
an3.1:4.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Thus, monks, you should train yourselves:
an3.1:4.2 | S: ‘We will shun the three things by which a fool is known, and we will proceed having undertaken the three things by which an astute person is known.’ | T: ‘We will avoid the three things that, endowed with which, one is to be recognized as a fool. We will undertake & maintain the three things that, endowed with which, one is to be recognized as a wise person.’
an3.1:4.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an3.1:4.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.20
an3.20:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a shopkeeper who has three factors soon acquires great and abundant wealth. | T: “Monks, a shopkeeper endowed with three factors will, in no long time, achieve greatness & abundance in terms of wealth.
an3.20:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.20:1.3 | S: It’s when a shopkeeper sees clearly, is indefatigable, and has supporters. | T: There is the case where a shopkeeper has a good eye, is astute, & is consummate in his backing.
an3.20:1.4 | S: And how does a shopkeeper see clearly? | T: “And how does a shopkeeper have a good eye?
an3.20:1.5 | S: It’s when a shopkeeper knows of a product: | T: There is the case where a shopkeeper knows of an item:
an3.20:1.6 | S: ‘This product is bought at this price and is selling at this price. With this much investment, it’ll bring this much profit.’ | T: ‘This item can be bought at this price and sold at this price; the cost will be this much, and the profit this much.’
an3.20:1.7 | S: That’s how a shopkeeper sees clearly. | T: This is how a shopkeeper has a good eye.
an3.20:2.1 | S: And how is a shopkeeper indefatigable? | T: “And how is a shopkeeper astute?
an3.20:2.2 | S: It’s when a shopkeeper is skilled in buying and selling products. | T: There is the case where a shopkeeper is skilled in buying & selling an item.
an3.20:2.3 | S: That’s how a shopkeeper is indefatigable. | T: This is how a shopkeeper is astute.
an3.20:3.1 | S: And how does a shopkeeper have supporters? | T: “And how is a shopkeeper consummate in his backing?
an3.20:3.2 | S: It’s when rich, affluent, and wealthy householders or householders’ children know of him: | T: There is the case where householders or householders’ sons know of a shopkeeper,
an3.20:3.3 | S: ‘This good shopkeeper sees clearly and is indefatigable. They are capable of providing for their wives and children, and paying us back from time to time.’ | T: ‘This shopkeeper has a good eye & is astute. He is capable of supporting his wife & children and repaying our investment at regular intervals.’
an3.20:3.4 | S: They deposit money with the shopkeeper, saying: | T: They offer him wealth, [saying,]
an3.20:3.5 | S: ‘With this, my good shopkeeper, earn money to provide for your wives and children, and pay us back from time to time.’ | T: ‘Here, my friend, having made wealth from this, support your wife & children and repay us at regular intervals.’
an3.20:3.6 | S: That’s how a shopkeeper has supporters. | T: This is how a shopkeeper is consummate in his backing.
an3.20:3.7 | S: A shopkeeper who has these three factors soon acquires great and abundant wealth. | T: “A shopkeeper endowed with these three factors will, in no long time, achieve greatness & abundance in terms of wealth.
an3.20:4.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with three qualities soon acquires great and abundant skillful qualities. | T: “Monks, a monk endowed with three factors will, in no long time, achieve greatness & abundance in terms of skillful qualities.
an3.20:4.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.20:4.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant sees clearly, is indefatigable, and has supporters. | T: There is the case where a monk has a good eye, is astute, & is consummate in his backing.
an3.20:4.4 | S: And how does a mendicant see clearly? | T: “And how does a monk have a good eye?
an3.20:4.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’… ‘This is the origination of stress’… ‘This is the cessation of stress’… ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an3.20:4.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant sees clearly. | T: This is how a monk has a good eye.
an3.20:5.1 | S: And how is a mendicant indefatigable? | T: “And how is a monk astute?
an3.20:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a monk keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities and taking on skillful qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
an3.20:5.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is indefatigable. | T: This is how a monk is astute.
an3.20:6.1 | S: And how does a mendicant have supporters? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in his backing?
an3.20:6.2 | S: It’s when from time to time a mendicant goes up to those mendicants who are very learned—inheritors of the heritage, who have memorized the teachings, the monastic law, and the outlines—and asks them questions: | T: There is the case where a monk—approaching at regular intervals those monks who are learned, to whom the tradition has come down, who have memorized the suttas, memorized the Vinaya, memorized the mātikas [lists of Dhamma topics]—asks & questions them,
an3.20:6.3 | S: ‘Why, sir, does it say this? What does that mean?’ | T: ‘How is this, venerable sirs? What is the meaning of this?’
an3.20:6.4 | S: Those venerables reveal what is hidden, clarify what is unclear, and dispel doubt regarding the many doubtful matters. | T: Those venerable ones make open what isn’t open, make plain what isn’t plain, dispel doubt on various doubtful points.
an3.20:6.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant has supporters. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in his backing.
an3.20:6.6 | S: A mendicant with these three qualities soon acquires great and abundant skillful qualities.” | T: “Monks, a monk endowed with these three factors will, in no long time, achieve greatness & abundance in terms of skillful qualities.”
an3.20:6.7 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.20:6.8 | P: Paṭhamabhāṇavāro niṭṭhito. | T: (none)
an3.20:6.9 | P: Rathakāravaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
an3.20:7.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an3.20:7.1 | P: Ñāto sāraṇīyo bhikkhu, | T: (none)
an3.20:7.2 | P: cakkavattī sacetano; | T: (none)
an3.20:7.3 | P: Apaṇṇakattā devo ca, | T: (none)
an3.20:7.4 | P: duve pāpaṇikena cāti. | T: (none)

## an3.22
an3.22:1.1 | S: “These three patients are found in the world. | T: “There are these three types of sick people to be found existing in the world.
an3.22:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.22:1.3 | S: In some cases a patient won’t recover from an illness, regardless of whether or not they get suitable food and medicines, and a capable carer. | T: “There is the case of the sick person who—regardless of whether he does or does not receive amenable food, regardless of whether he does or does not receive amenable medicine, regardless of whether he does or does not receive proper nursing—will not recover from that illness.
an3.22:2.1 | S: In some cases a patient will recover from an illness, regardless of whether or not they get suitable food and medicines, and a capable carer. | T: There is the case of the sick person who—regardless of whether he does or does not receive amenable food, regardless of whether he does or does not receive amenable medicine, regardless of whether he does or does not receive proper nursing—will recover from that illness.
an3.22:3.1 | S: In some cases a patient can recover from an illness, but only if they get suitable food and medicines, and a capable carer, and not if they don’t get these things. | T: There is the case of the sick person who will recover from that illness if he receives amenable food, amenable medicine, & proper nursing, but not if he doesn’t.
an3.22:4.1 | S: Now, it’s for the sake of the last patient—who will recover only if they get suitable food and medicines, and a capable carer—that food, medicines, and a carer are prescribed. | T: “Now, it is because of the sick person who will recover from that illness if he receives amenable food, amenable medicine, & proper nursing—but not if he doesn’t—that food for the sick has been allowed, medicine for the sick has been allowed, nursing for the sick has been allowed.
an3.22:4.2 | S: But also, for the sake of this patient, the other patients should be looked after. | T: And it is because there is this sort of sick person that the other sorts of sick persons are to be nursed as well [on the chance that they may actually turn out to need and benefit from such nursing].
an3.22:4.3 | S: These are the three kinds of patients found in the world. | T: “These are the three types of sick people to be found existing in the world.
an3.22:5.1 | S: In the same way, these three individuals similar to patients are found in the world. | T: “In the same way, these three types of people, like the three types of sick people, are to be found existing in the world.
an3.22:5.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.22:5.3 | S: Some people don’t step into the sure path with regard to skillful qualities, regardless of whether or not they get to see a Realized One, and to hear the teaching and training that he proclaims. | T: “There is the case of the person who—regardless of whether he does or doesn’t get to see the Tathāgata, regardless of whether he does or doesn’t get to hear the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathāgata—will not alight on the lawfulness, the rightness of skillful qualities.
an3.22:6.1 | S: Some people do step into the sure path with regard to skillful qualities, regardless of whether or not they get to see a Realized One, and to hear the teaching and training that he proclaims. | T: There is the case of the person who—regardless of whether he does or doesn’t get to see the Tathāgata, regardless of whether he does or doesn’t get to hear the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathāgata—will alight on the lawfulness, the rightness of skillful qualities.
an3.22:7.1 | S: Some people can step into the sure path with regard to skillful qualities, but only if they get to see a Realized One, and to hear the teaching and training that he proclaims, and not when they don’t get those things. | T: There is the case of the person who will alight on the lawfulness, the rightness of skillful qualities if he gets to see the Tathāgata and gets to hear the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathāgata, but not if he doesn’t.
an3.22:8.1 | S: Now, it’s for the sake of this last individual that teaching the Dhamma is prescribed, that is, the one who can step into the sure path with regard to skillful qualities, but only if they get to see a Realized One, and to hear the teaching and training that he proclaims. | T: “Now, it is because of the person who will alight on the lawfulness, the rightness of skillful qualities if he gets to see the Tathāgata and gets to hear the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathāgata—but not if he doesn’t—that the teaching of the Dhamma has been allowed.
an3.22:8.2 | S: But also, for the sake of this individual, the other people should be taught Dhamma. | T: And it is because there is this sort of person that the other sorts of persons are to be taught the Dhamma as well [on the chance that they may actually turn out to need and benefit from the teaching].
an3.22:8.3 | S: These are the three individuals similar to patients found in the world.” | T: “These are the three types of people, like the three types of sick people, to be found existing in the world.”
an3.22:8.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.24
an3.24:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these three individuals are very helpful to another. | T: “Monks, these three persons are one’s great benefactors.
an3.24:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.24:1.3 | S: The individual who has enabled you to go for refuge to the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha. | T: “The person through whom one has gone to the Buddha for refuge, has gone to the Dhamma for refuge, has gone to the Saṅgha for refuge:
an3.24:1.4 | S: This individual is very helpful to another. | T: This is one’s great benefactor.
an3.24:2.1 | S: Furthermore, the individual who has enabled you to truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: “Further, the person through whom one discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’… ‘This is the origination of stress’… ‘This is the cessation of stress’… ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress’:
an3.24:2.2 | S: This individual is very helpful to another. | T: This is one’s great benefactor.
an3.24:3.1 | S: Furthermore, the individual who has enabled you to realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom, and live having realized it with your own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: “Further, the person through whom one—with the ending of effluents—enters & remains in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized it for himself right in the here & now:
an3.24:3.2 | S: This individual is very helpful to another. | T: This is one’s great benefactor.
an3.24:3.3 | S: These are the three individuals who are very helpful to another individual. | T: “These three persons, monks, are one’s great benefactors.
an3.24:4.1 | S: And I say that there is no one more helpful to another than these three individuals. | T: And I tell you: One has no other benefactor greater than these three.
an3.24:4.2 | S: And I also say that it’s not easy to repay these three individuals by bowing down to them, rising up for them, greeting them with cupped palms, and observing proper etiquette for them; or by providing them with robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick.” | T: And I tell you, too, that it’s not easy for one to repay these three benefactors by bowing down, by rising up in greeting, by raising one’s hands palm-to-palm in front of the heart, by performing services in line with seniority, or by presenting them with robes, almsfood, lodging, or medicinal requisites used for curing the sick.”
an3.24:4.3 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.2
an3.2:1.1 | S: “A fool is characterized by their deeds, and an astute person is characterized by their deeds, for wisdom shines in its traces. | T: “Monks, a fool is characterized by his/her actions. A wise person is characterized by his/her actions. It is through the activities of one’s life that one’s discernment shines.
an3.2:1.2 | S: A fool is known by three things. | T: “A person endowed with three things is to be recognized as a fool.
an3.2:1.3 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.2:1.4 | S: Bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, mental misconduct.
an3.2:1.5 | S: These are the three things by which a fool is known. | T: A person endowed with these three things is to be recognized as a fool.
an3.2:2.1 | S: An astute person is known by three things. | T: “A person endowed with three things is to be recognized as a wise person.
an3.2:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.2:2.3 | S: Good conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Good bodily conduct, good verbal conduct, good mental conduct.
an3.2:2.4 | S: These are the three things by which an astute person is known. | T: A person endowed with these three things is to be recognized as a wise person.
an3.2:3.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Thus, monks, you should train yourselves:
an3.2:3.2 | S: ‘We will shun the three things by which a fool is known, and we will proceed having undertaken the three things by which an astute person is known.’ | T: ‘We will avoid the three things that, endowed with which, one is to be recognized as a fool. We will undertake & maintain the three things that, endowed with which, one is to be recognized as a wise person.’
an3.2:3.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an3.2:3.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.33
an3.33:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
an3.33:1.2 | S: “Maybe I’ll teach Dhamma in brief, | T: “Sāriputta, I could teach the Dhamma in brief,
an3.33:1.3 | S: maybe in detail, | T: I could teach the Dhamma in detail,
an3.33:1.4 | S: maybe both in brief and in detail. | T: I could teach the Dhamma in brief & in detail,
an3.33:1.5 | S: But it’s hard to find anyone who understands.” | T: but those who would understand the Dhamma are hard to find.”
an3.33:1.6 | S: “Now is the time, Blessed One! Now is the time, Holy One! | T: “This is the time, Blessed One, this is the time, One Well Gone,
an3.33:1.7 | S: Let the Buddha teach Dhamma in brief, in detail, and both in brief and in detail. | T: that the Blessed One should teach the Dhamma in brief, should teach the Dhamma in detail, should teach the Dhamma in brief & in detail.
an3.33:1.8 | S: There will be those who understand the teaching!” | T: There will be those who would understand.”
an3.33:2.1 | S: “So, Sāriputta, you should train like this: | T: “Then, Sāriputta, you should train yourselves:
an3.33:2.2 | S: ‘There’ll be no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body; and no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit externally for all signs; and we’ll live having achieved the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit are no more.’ | T: ‘There will be no I-making or mine-making conceit-obsession with regard to this conscious body. There will be no I-making or mine-making conceit-obsession with regard to all external themes [topics of concentration]. We will enter & remain in the awareness-release & discernment-release where there is no I-making or my-making conceit-obsession for one entering & remaining in it.’
an3.33:2.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.
an3.33:3.1 | S: When a mendicant has no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body; and no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit externally for all signs; and they live having attained the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit are no more— | T: When there is in a monk no I-making or my-making conceit-obsession with regard to this conscious body, no I-making or my-making conceit-obsession with regard to all external themes, and when he enters & remains in the awareness-release & discernment-release where there is no I-making or my-making conceit-obsession for one entering & remaining in it,
an3.33:3.2 | S: they’re called | T: he is called
